Q: How can I restrict property to accept only components with specific props? A: <p>The main problem lies in the FilteredTableColumn.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>interface FilteredTableColumn&lt;T extends FilterComponentProps = FilterComponentProps&gt; {
    accessor: string;
    filter?: {
        component: ComponentType&lt;T&gt;,
        props: T;
    }
}
interface FilteredTableProps {
    columns: FilteredTableColumn[];
}
</code></pre>
<p>The FilteredTableColumn expects to receive a generic parameter T. However, <code>FilteredTableProps.columns:FilteredTableColumn[]</code> does not pass the specified parameter. In that case, FilteredTableColumn defaults to:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>interface FilteredTableColumn&lt;FilterComponentProps&gt; {
    accessor: string;
    filter?: {
        component: ComponentType&lt;FilterComponentProps&gt;,
        props: FilterComponentProps;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>So when you define the type of the table and use it in practice, the type of the &quot;cols&quot; variable won't match. The actual type of cols is <code>FilteredTableColumn&lt;StringFilterComponentProps&gt;</code> but the table expects the type of columns to be <code>FilteredTableColumn&lt;FilterComponentProps&gt;</code>  This led to this TypeScript error.</p>
<pre><code>const FilteredTable: FunctionComponent&lt;FilteredTableProps&gt; = (props) =&gt; (
    &lt;table&gt;
        {props.columns.map(column =&gt; 
            &lt;td key={column.accessor}&gt;
                {column.filter &amp;&amp; 
                    &lt;column.filter.component
                        {...column.filter.props}
                    /&gt;
                }
            &lt;/td&gt;
        )}
    &lt;/table&gt;
);

const cols: FilteredTableColumn&lt;StringFilterComponentProps&gt;[] = [{
    accessor: &quot;name&quot;,
    filter: { component: StringFilterComponent, 
        props: { mask: &quot;test&quot;, accesor: &quot;name&quot; }
    }
}];
</code></pre>
<p>The solution is to modify the <code>FilteredTableProps</code> so that it can accept generic parameters.</p>
<pre><code>interface FilteredTableProps&lt;T extends FilterComponentProps = FilterComponentProps&gt; {
    columns: FilteredTableColumn&lt;T&gt;[];
}

const FilteredTable = &lt;T extends FilterComponentProps&gt;(props: FilteredTableProps&lt;T&gt;) =&gt; (
    &lt;table&gt;
        {props.columns.map(column =&gt; 
            &lt;td key={column.accessor}&gt;
                {column.filter &amp;&amp; 
                    &lt;column.filter.component
                        {...column.filter.props}
                    /&gt;
                }
            &lt;/td&gt;
        )}
    &lt;/table&gt;
);
</code></pre>

Q: JavaScript loop through JSON array? A: <p>A short solution using <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/map" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>map</code></a> and an <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/Arrow_functions" rel="nofollow noreferrer">arrow function</a></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var data = [{
  "id": "1",
  "msg": "hi",
  "tid": "2013-05-05 23:35",
  "fromWho": "hello1@email.se"
}, {
  "id": "2",
  "msg": "there",
  "tid": "2013-05-05 23:45",
  "fromWho": "hello2@email.se"
}, {
      "msg": "abcde",
      "tid": "2013-06-06 23:46",
      "fromWho": "hello3@email.se"
    }];
data.map((item, i) =&gt; console.log(`Index: ${i}`, {id: item.id}));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>And to cover the cases when the property <code>&quot;id&quot;</code> is not present use <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/filter" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>filter</code></a>:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var data = [{
  "id": "1",
  "msg": "hi",
  "tid": "2013-05-05 23:35",
  "fromWho": "hello1@email.se"
}, {
  "id": "2",
  "msg": "there",
  "tid": "2013-05-05 23:45",
  "fromWho": "hello2@email.se"
}, {
  "msg": "abcde",
  "tid": "2013-06-06 23:46",
  "fromWho": "hello3@email.se"
}];

data.filter(item=&gt;item.hasOwnProperty('id'))
                .map((item, i) =&gt; console.log(`Index: ${i}`, {id: item.id}));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
